          "source_topic_bucket_id": null,
          "objectives": [
            {
              "id": 30756,
              "objective_key": "331cb75a-045d-49b0-91cd-bc0e2b701190",
              "objective_text": "By the end of this lesson the Chemistry Advanced Subsidiary level Learner should be able to: \nwrite and construct balanced equations, including state symbols",
              "order_index": 1
            },
            {
              "id": 30757,
              "objective_key": "c36c652f-0131-455e-a41e-73567e7657d4",
              "objective_text": "By the end of this lesson the Chemistry Advanced Subsidiary level Learner should be able to: perform calculations, including use of the mole concept, involving:\n- reacting masses (from formulae and equations)\n- volumes of gases at r.t.p and s.t.p (e.g. in the burning of hydrocarbons)\n- volumes and concentrations of solutions\nNOTE:\n- Answers to calculations should reflect the number of significant figures given or\nasked for in the question. Significant figures should neither be lost unnecessarily nor\nused beyond what is justified (see also Practical Assessment, Paper 3, Display of\ncalculation and reasoning, in Annexe A2)\n- Vm = 22.4 dm3 mol −1\nat s.t.p\n- Vm = 24.0 dm3 mol −1\nat r.t.p\n\n(where s.t.p is expressed as 101 kPa, approximately,\n",
              "order_index": 2
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "id": 6554,
          "topicId": "637b29b7-3c36-4bf3-8237-89fb3d765864",
          "section_id": 2459,
          "title": "(L1)Ionic bonding",
          "previous_knowledge": null,
          "private_teacher_notes": null,
          "estimated_time_to_mastery": null,
          "order_index": null,
          "source_topic_bucket_id": null,
          "objectives": [
            {
              "id": 30927,
              "objective_key": "f2d58ce4-f3f5-49d9-9789-efe3aeb7b744",
              "objective_text": "By the end of this lesson the Chemistry Advanced Subsidiary level Learner should be able to: \ndescribe ionic bonding, as in sodium chloride, magnesium oxide and calcium fluoride,\nincluding the use of „dot-and-cross‟ diagrams",
              "order_index": 1
            },
            {
              "id": 30928,
              "objective_key": "b87c11d3-f8c6-490d-9b0b-8add4dccfb81",
              "objective_text": "By the end of this lesson the Chemistry Advanced Subsidiary level Learner should be able to: \ndescribe the electrostatic forces in ionic compounds as being attraction between\noppositely charged ions",
              "order_index": 2
            },
            {
              "id": 30929,
              "objective_key": "1e927995-2d02-471c-97aa-bfdbfcfe82a2",
              "objective_text": "By the end of this lesson the Chemistry Advanced Subsidiary level Learner should be able to: describe, interpret and predict the effect of ionic bonding on physical properties\n(including physical state, melting point, boiling point, solubility)\n",
              "order_index": 3
            },
            {
              "id": 30930,
              "objective_key": "24cff215-12d1-43ed-8ad2-e7e831ca0291",
              "objective_text": "By the end of this lesson the Chemistry Advanced Subsidiary level Learner should be able to: explain the electrical conductivity of ionic compounds when solid, molten or in solution\n",
              "order_index": 4
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "id": 6555,
          "topicId": "993e2bd1-f34d-44ec-9a87-7a45ba5217c0",
          "section_id": 2459,
          "title": "(L1)Covalent bonding and co-ordinate (dative covalent) including shapes of simple molecules",
          "previous_knowledge": null,
          "private_teacher_notes": null,
          "estimated_time_to_mastery": null,
          "order_index": null,
          "source_topic_bucket_id": null,
          "objectives": [
            {
              "id": 30935,
              "objective_key": "13b008cc-6198-4a59-9c1d-0c5ebe933585",
              "objective_text": "By the end of this lesson the Chemistry Advanced Subsidiary level Learner should be able to: describe, including the use of „dot-and-cross‟ diagrams:\n- covalent bonding, including molecules such as hydrogen, chlorine, oxygen, nitrogen,\nhydrogen chloride, carbon dioxide, methane, ethene\n- dative covalent (co-ordinate) bonding, including in the formation of the ammonium\nion and in the Al2Cl6 molecule, CO, NH4\n+\n, H3O\n+\n",
              "order_index": 1
            },
            {
              "id": 30936,
              "objective_key": "7bc959dc-180a-4cfa-b4e7-c544e228e2d2",
              "objective_text": "By the end of this lesson the Chemistry Advanced Subsidiary level Learner should be able to: explain the shapes of, and bond angles in, molecules by using the\n- qualitative model of electron-pair repulsion (including lone pairs), using as simple\nexamples: BF3 (trigonal planar), CO2 (linear), CH4 (tetrahedral), NH3 (pyramidal),\nH2O (non-linear/v-shaped), SF6 (octahedral), PF5 (trigonal bipyramidal)\n",
              "order_index": 2
            },
            {
              "id": 30937,
              "objective_key": "3e0c56ff-e1b0-47e4-b276-56ecd9b4effa",
              "objective_text": "By the end of this lesson the Chemistry Advanced Subsidiary level Learner should be able to: \nuse the electron-pair repulsion theory to predict the shapes of, and bond angles in\nother molecules analogous to those specified in bullet 2 above (see section 4.1.2)",
              "order_index": 3
            },
            {
              "id": 30938,
              "objective_key": "36bb0cd6-1721-4de8-a5aa-368062802a66",
              "objective_text": "By the end of this lesson the Chemistry Advanced Subsidiary level Learner should be able to: \ndescribe, interpret and predict the effect of covalent bonding on physical properties\n(including physical state, melting point, boiling point, solubility)",
              "order_index": 4
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "id": 6557,
          "topicId": "eb2316f0-cfa6-4b74-9f0c-956157d8e0d6",
          "section_id": 2459,
          "title": "(L1) Electronegativity",
          "previous_knowledge": null,
          "private_teacher_notes": null,
          "estimated_time_to_mastery": null,
          "order_index": null,
          "source_topic_bucket_id": null,
          "objectives": [
            {
              "id": 30944,
              "objective_key": "4bb3edb8-5e36-4440-be56-17145a4b0e99",
              "objective_text": "By the end of this lesson the Chemistry Advanced Subsidiary level Learner should be able to: explain the concept of electronegativity\n",
              "order_index": 1
            },
            {
              "id": 30945,
              "objective_key": "ba6ef9ab-ea80-4c93-a99f-f4682220a41d",
              "objective_text": "By the end of this lesson the Chemistry Advanced Subsidiary level Learner should be able to: explain, in terms of electronegativity, the properties of molecules such as bond polarity\nthe dipole moments of molecules and the behaviour of oxides with water\n",
              "order_index": 2
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "id": 6589,
          "topicId": "e1a5a3bc-1df5-4787-b8c2-c6efcbe342e9",
          "section_id": 2459,
          "title": "(L1)Intermolecular forces, electronegativity and bond properties",
          "previous_knowledge": null,
          "private_teacher_notes": null,
          "estimated_time_to_mastery": null,
          "order_index": null,
          "source_topic_bucket_id": null,
          "objectives": [
            {
              "id": 31101,
              "objective_key": "6b3c27eb-269e-4b57-9814-d46354d5ccda",
              "objective_text": "By the end of this lesson the Chemistry Advanced Subsidiary level Learner should be able to: describe the differences between bonding (intramolecular) and intermolecular\nforces in covalent molecules\n",
              "order_index": 1
            },
            {
              "id": 31102,
              "objective_key": "4c205149-0ae2-418e-97f2-b8d517ea7e07",
              "objective_text": "By the end of this lesson the Chemistry Advanced Subsidiary level Learner should be able to: describe that the electrostatic forces in ionic compounds are much stronger than\nintermolecular forces in covalent molecules\n",
              "order_index": 2
            },
            {
              "id": 31103,
              "objective_key": "e0963d6d-3887-4e44-9194-ea066ebc3f91",
              "objective_text": "By the end of this lesson the Chemistry Advanced Subsidiary level Learner should be able to: describe that the bonding (intramolecular ) forces are much stronger than\nintermolecular forces in covalent bonding\n",
              "order_index": 3
            },
            {
              "id": 31104,
              "objective_key": "06cb6625-d504-4843-be56-2b62fd07f810",
              "objective_text": "By the end of this lesson the Chemistry Advanced Subsidiary level Learner should be able to: \ndescribe the difference in volatility, solubility, melting/boiling points and electrical\nconductivity between ionic and covalent compounds in terms of forces of\nattraction (knowledge of hybridisation is not required)",
              "order_index": 4
            },
            {
              "id": 31105,
              "objective_key": "7a492006-62ac-48c3-86df-f5d944cfe62e",
              "objective_text": "By the end of this lesson the Chemistry Advanced Subsidiary level Learner should be able to: describe intermolecular forces in covalent compounds and noble gases: induced\ndipole (van der Waals) forces, permanent dipole forces and hydrogen bonding\n",
              "order_index": 5
            },
            {
              "id": 31106,
              "objective_key": "0db83f10-5c79-4d95-ac06-5556283789cc",
              "objective_text": "By the end of this lesson the Chemistry Advanced Subsidiary level Learner should be able to: explain differences in physical properties in terms of intermolecular forces,\nincluding:\n- noble gases, Br2(l),\n- HCl(g), CHCl3(l),\n- ammonia and water as simple examples of molecules containing N–H and\nO–H groups\n",
              "order_index": 6
            },
            {
              "id": 31107,
              "objective_key": "8962a80c-f933-471b-9fbe-83c29fa7f31a",
              "objective_text": "By the end of this lesson the Chemistry Advanced Subsidiary level Learner should be able to: state that when giant covalent substances melt, covalent bonds break and when\nsimple molecular substances melt or boil, forces between molecules weaken\n",
              "order_index": 7
            },
            {
              "id": 31108,
              "objective_key": "09de5807-1502-424b-9e8a-17396190b5cf",
              "objective_text": "By the end of this lesson the Chemistry Advanced Subsidiary level Learner should be able to: describe hydrogen bonds as the strongest intermolecular force yet still weak\ncompared with covalent bonding\n",
              "order_index": 8
            },
            {
              "id": 31109,
              "objective_key": "31fc34f4-773e-47f2-8907-9e2d2be3fa56",
              "objective_text": "By the end of this lesson the Chemistry Advanced Subsidiary level Learner should be able to: \nexplain the effect of hydrogen bonding on the physical properties of substances,\nincluding ice, water, methane, ammonia, hydrogen fluoride and methanol (for\nexample, boiling and melting points, viscosity and surface tension)",
              "order_index": 9
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "id": 6613,
          "topicId": "29d33c66-416a-4f0a-8d44-ccbb91ee5606",
          "section_id": 2459,
          "title": "(L1)Metallic bonding",
          "previous_knowledge": null,
          "private_teacher_notes": null,
          "estimated_time_to_mastery": null,
          "order_index": null,
          "source_topic_bucket_id": null,
          "objectives": [
            {
              "id": 31161,
              "objective_key": "f43f077c-c81e-49c3-b68e-3dcda474abf4",
              "objective_text": "By the end of this lesson the Chemistry Advanced Subsidiary level Learner should be able to: \ndescribe metallic bonding in terms of a lattice of positive ions surrounded by\ndelocalised electrons",
              "order_index": 1
            },
            {
              "id": 31162,
              "objective_key": "051a9738-c08a-4212-b8c2-558416466f40",
              "objective_text": "By the end of this lesson the Chemistry Advanced Subsidiary level Learner should be able to: describe and explain the electrical conductivity, melting point and malleability/\nductility of metals in terms of their structure and bonding\n",
              "order_index": 2
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "id": 6616,
          "topicId": "ca3ae110-4495-4cf4-866c-1ab6da400827",
          "section_id": 2459,
          "title": "(L1)Bonding, structure and physical properties",
          "previous_knowledge": null,
          "private_teacher_notes": null,
          "estimated_time_to_mastery": null,
          "order_index": null,
          "source_topic_bucket_id": null,
          "objectives": [
            {
              "id": 31168,
              "objective_key": "9a93e2ff-8159-4d98-bf27-aa2a141ce01e",
              "objective_text": "By the end of this lesson the Chemistry Advanced Subsidiary level Learner should be able to: describe, in simple terms, the lattice structure of a crystalline solid which is:\n- ionic, including sodium chloride, magnesium oxide\n- simple molecular, including iodine and the fullerene allotropes of carbon\n(C60 and nanotubes only)\n- giant molecular, including silicon(IV) oxide and the graphite, diamond and\ngraphene allotropes of carbon\n- hydrogen-bonded, including ice\n- metallic, including copper\n",
              "order_index": 1
            },
            {
              "id": 31169,
              "objective_key": "091bc4d8-8886-4214-b40a-aca6a3d09b84",
              "objective_text": "By the end of this lesson the Chemistry Advanced Subsidiary level Learner should be able to: explain the relative energy and temperature required for changes of state in terms\nof the relative energy needed to break bonds (with reference to ionic, giant\ncovalent and metallic substances) and to weaken intermolecular forces (with\nreference to simple covalent structures)\n",
              "order_index": 2
            },
            {
              "id": 31170,
              "objective_key": "2a39645d-9ed5-4af2-98f5-6904bd8456e3",
              "objective_text": "By the end of this lesson the Chemistry Advanced Subsidiary level Learner should be able to: describe, interpret and predict the effect of different types of structure and bonding\nionic bonding, covalent bonding (giant and simple structures), hydrogen bonding,\nother intermolecular interactions, metallic bonding on the physical properties of\nsubstances, including electrical conductivity\n",
              "order_index": 3
            },
            {
              "id": 31171,
              "objective_key": "f56a5e84-7a88-491a-800f-effd14e212c8",
              "objective_text": "By the end of this lesson the Chemistry Advanced Subsidiary level Learner should be able to: deduce the type of bonding and structure present from given information\n",
              "order_index": 4
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "id": 6619,
          "topicId": "c606eb4f-2919-4b48-a2dd-67547206aeef",
          "section_id": 2459,
          "title": "(L1)The ideal gas and real gases and pV = nRT",
          "previous_knowledge": null,
          "private_teacher_notes": null,
          "estimated_time_to_mastery": null,
          "order_index": null,
          "source_topic_bucket_id": null,
          "objectives": [
            {
              "id": 31179,
              "objective_key": "e7081f83-b0e3-479d-a714-6bdf42a8d928",
              "objective_text": "By the end of this lesson the Chemistry Advanced Subsidiary level Learner should be able to: \nstate the basic assumptions of the kinetic theory as applied to an ideal gas",
              "order_index": 1
            },
            {
              "id": 31180,
              "objective_key": "89cb4c56-7669-49d1-aa00-a8e51907bdf6",
              "objective_text": "By the end of this lesson the Chemistry Advanced Subsidiary level Learner should be able to: explain qualitatively in terms of intermolecular forces and molecular size:\n- the conditions necessary for a gas to approach ideal behaviour\n- the limitations of ideality at very high pressures and very low temperatures\n",
              "order_index": 2
            },
            {
              "id": 31181,
              "objective_key": "8cba01d5-db48-46f2-89ce-604d9970379c",
              "objective_text": "By the end of this lesson the Chemistry Advanced Subsidiary level Learner should be able to: recall and use the general gas equation pV = nRT in calculations, including the\ndetermination of Mr (NOTE: R = 8.31 J K−1 mol−1\n)\n",
              "order_index": 3
            }
          ]
        }
      ]
